Debir, formerly called Kiriath Sepher, meaning “City of Books,” had been conquered during Joshua’s southern campaign and was later given to the priests. Its earlier reputation recalls the importance of knowledge and instruction among God’s people. As a priestly city, Debir stood as a reminder that Israel’s life in the land was to be governed by the revealed wisdom of God.

